I've revised the stage-2 plan for the plan-checker's second-round finding and sent it the new commit, `cc55daf2180e21a015a453b2a1a7c6b5819f39e4`. That is now the plan head. No checks were run and nothing was pushed.

The finding was right: a page can show a fact in the browser that its plain text lacks, so my cheap pre-check could have thrown away a page with a valid candidate. The pre-check now only decides the order pages are rendered in and never drops one:

- **When the budget covers every page:** every search-result page is rendered and no pre-check is spent. Nothing can be missed.
- **When it doesn't:** the pre-check ranks pages, using the search snippet first and then a 1-unit plain fetch. Pages likely to hold a value are rendered first, but pages that look empty are still rendered while budget remains.
- **Tests:** the test that would have locked in the old behaviour is replaced by three:
  - every page is rendered when the budget allows;
  - a fact visible only in the browser is found when affordable;
  - a page that looked empty is still rendered while budget remains.

One gap remains, now recorded as conflict C-S3 and waiting on the lead. Rendering with Nimble alone costs 3 units a page. After searches, research and the reserve for research-cited pages, that leaves room for roughly 9–15 search-result pages per build. The sample companies have 13–24 pages each. For larger companies, a fact visible only in the browser on a page the budget never reaches can be missed, whatever method is used. The options I put to the lead:

- **(a)** accept it as a documented limitation;
- **(b)** bring in Kernel browser sessions at 1 unit a page whenever a company has more pages than Nimble can afford;
- **(c)** drop the pre-check and render in search order.

I recommended (a), plus (b) if the Nimble-only test run shows missed values. The plan-checker is taking C-S3 to the lead with its verdict.

The plan-checker passed the stage-2 plan at `e80cc34d684e8d79972a01e4b2208d83799bd2a1`. The only open item, how pages are read, now follows the lead's ruling:
- no cheap check can skip a page;
- pages are rendered in priority order until the 80-unit budget runs out;
- pages the budget never reaches are logged as a documented limit.

The plan-checker also confirmed these still hold:
- the reserve for pages cited by research;
- the lead's four decisions;
- the stage-1 import limitation;
- the sign-in boundary for browser pages;
- the added proofs.

The plan-checker reports the verdict to the lead, so I acknowledged without replying. The stage-2 plan is final. No checks were run and nothing was pushed.

